PR #156  overall: bug_confirmed
Spec: Mismatched Zilsd alignment flags cause the compiler to emit 64-bit memory accesses at 4-byte alignment targeting hardware that requires 8-byte natural alignment, risking silent data corruption or unexpected traps.
  [BUG] lib/sbi/sbi_trap_ldst.c:81-90  sbi_trap_emulate_load
      INSN_MATCH_LD (0x3003) decode branch is gated entirely on #if __riscv_xlen == 64 (L84). On RV32+Zilsd, the Zilsd LD instruction shares the identical 0x3003 opcode, but the entire branch is compiled out. The instruction falls through the else-if chain to sbi_trap_redirect() at L151, which bounces the unhandled trap to S-mode. S-mode likewise has no Zilsd misaligned emulation, so the trap is either re-delegated or kills the supervisor — a DoS vector. The security consequence is that on strict-align hardware (where Zilsd 64-bit accesses at 4-byte alignment trap), every such access is unrecoverable. Even on word-align-tolerant hardware, if the access happens to be truly misaligned (< 4 bytes), M-mode cannot emulate it.
  [BUG] lib/sbi/sbi_trap_ldst.c:205-212  sbi_trap_emulate_store
      Mirror image of the LD decode bug. INSN_MATCH_SD (0x3023) decode is inside #if __riscv_xlen == 64 (L209). On RV32+Zilsd, Zilsd SD shares this opcode but the branch is compiled out. Misaligned Zilsd stores trap to M-mode, fail decode, and get redirected to S-mode via sbi_trap_redirect() — same DoS outcome as the load side. Every misaligned Zilsd 64-bit store on RV32 is unrecoverable regardless of the underlying emulator correctness.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_trap.c:332-340  sbi_trap_handler
      The trap dispatcher correctly routes CAUSE_MISALIGNED_LOAD (L332-335) and CAUSE_MISALIGNED_STORE (L337-340) to sbi_misaligned_load_handler() and sbi_misaligned_store_handler() respectively. The routing logic itself has no bug — it is the single funnel point that all misaligned traps pass through. The risk is architectural: there is no intermediate check for whether the trapped instruction is a Zilsd opcode that the downstream handlers cannot decode on RV32. If such a check existed here (e.g., inspecting mtval for the opcode before dispatch), the handler could fail gracefully (e.g., emulating byte-by-byte directly) rather than bouncing to S-mode. Currently safe in the sense that it dispatches correctly; the failure occurs downstream.
  [OK] lib/sbi/sbi_hart.c:224-231  delegate_traps
      Boot-time delegation explicitly excludes CAUSE_MISALIGNED_LOAD and CAUSE_MISALIGNED_STORE from the delegated exception set. Only MISALIGNED_FETCH, BREAKPOINT, USER_ECALL, and page faults are delegated (L225-231). This is the correct architectural decision: OpenSBI intends to handle all misaligned load/store traps in M-mode. The bug is not in the delegation policy but in the M-mode handler's inability to decode Zilsd opcodes on RV32. The delegation decision is what makes the decode bug impactful — if these traps were delegated to S-mode, the S-mode kernel (which also lacks Zilsd misaligned emulation logic) would face the same problem, but M-mode would be innocent.
  [OK] lib/sbi/sbi_trap_ldst.c:271-289  sbi_misaligned_ld_emulator
