  [OK] lib/sbi/sbi_trap_ldst.c:296-314  sbi_misaligned_st_emulator
      Mirror of the load emulator — byte-by-byte store via sbi_store_u8() (L304-312), correct fault handling and tinst fixup. Would correctly emulate Zilsd 8-byte misaligned stores if reachable. The reachability gap is the same upstream decode bug in sbi_trap_emulate_store() at L209-211.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_hart.c:368-399  sbi_hart_ext
      The ISA extension table `sbi_hart_ext[]` has no entry for Zilsd. sbi_hart_has_extension() can never return true for Zilsd, so no runtime logic can condition on Zilsd presence — e.g., to enable alternative decode paths, to selectively delegate misaligned traps, or to advertise Zilsd support to S-mode via SBI_EXT_HART_EXT. This is a blocking prerequisite for any feature-complete fix: even after fixing the decode gates, the fix logic has no standard way to discover whether the current hart actually supports Zilsd. Currently safe only because nothing queries for Zilsd yet, but any future fix that adds a `has_zilsd()` guard will silently return false on all harts.
  [OK] lib/sbi/sbi_fwft.c:121-131  fwft_set_misaligned_delegation
      MIS_DELEG (defined at L37) correctly bundles CAUSE_MISALIGNED_LOAD and CAUSE_MISALIGNED_STORE as an atomic unit: both are set or cleared together in MEDELEG via csr_set/csr_clear (L125, L127). This is an ISA-imposed limitation (MEDELEG has one bit per cause, with no sub-cause granularity), not a code bug. The all-or-nothing model means Zilsd 64-bit misaligned traps cannot be delegated independently of 32-bit misaligned traps. This is relevant if the fix strategy involves selective delegation — the hardware simply doesn't support it. The code correctly implements the only possible behavior given the ISA constraint.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_unpriv.c:81-114  sbi_load_u64 / sbi_store_u64
      DEFINE_UNPRIVILEGED_LOAD_FUNCTION(u64, ld) and DEFINE_UNPRIVILEGED_STORE_FUNCTION(u64, sd) are gated on #if __riscv_xlen == 64 (L81-85). On RV32 (L86-114), 64-bit unpriv accesses manually decompose into two sbi_load_u32/sbi_store_u32 calls. This means the firmware cannot use native Zilsd LD/SD instructions for 64-bit unprivileged emulation even when the hardware actually supports Zilsd. The fallback decomposition is functionally correct (two atomic 32-bit halves) but has a subtle atomicity gap: a concurrent writer could modify the high 32 bits between the two u32 loads, producing a torn 64-bit value. This is not a direct violation of the Zilsd alignment spec, but it means the firmware's internal emulation path for Zilsd-like operations lacks the atomicity that hardware Zilsd would provide.
  [OK] lib/sbi/sbi_illegal_insn.c:148-175  sbi_illegal_insn_handler
      The illegal-instruction handler dispatches through illegal_insn_table[] based on opcode bits (L174) with no Zilsd-specific awareness. This is correct behavior for genuinely illegal/unsupported instructions — on hardware without Zilsd support, a Zilsd LD or SD instruction correctly traps as illegal and gets handled generically. The handler's lack of Zilsd knowledge is not a bug because the Zilsd misaligned-access failure mode goes through the misaligned trap path (sbi_trap_handler L332-340), not through this handler. The two paths are architecturally distinct: Zilsd on Zilsd-capable hardware with strict alignment → misaligned trap → decode failure (bug). Zilsd on non-Zilsd hardware → illegal instruction trap → correct generic handling. No fix needed here.
  [RISK] include/sbi/riscv_encoding.h:887-903  (opcode definitions)
      INSN_MATCH_LD (0x3003) and INSN_MATCH_SD (0x3023) are defined unconditionally — no #if or comment distinguishes the RV64 meaning from the RV32+Zilsd meaning. On RV64 these encode native 64-bit LD/SD; on RV32+Zilsd the identical bit patterns encode Zilsd 64-bit memory operations. This creates a latent maintenance hazard: any developer modifying the decode logic may assume these macros are RV64-only and add new __riscv_xlen == 64 gates, compounding the existing bug. The definitions themselves are correct (the bit patterns are what they are), but the lack of any annotation or dual-context awareness makes it easy to write broken #if guards around their usage.
  [RISK] Makefile:392-394  (build system: strict-align)
      -mstrict-align is unconditionally appended to CFLAGS when the compiler supports it (detected at L203-204, applied at L392-394). This is correct for the firmware binary itself: OpenSBI should not generate misaligned accesses. However, the build system has no awareness of Zilsd-specific compiler flags (-mzilsd-word-align vs -mzilsd-strict-align) that control alignment assumptions for Zilsd code generation in S-mode payloads. The build system cannot detect or warn about a mismatch between the firmware's alignment policy and the S-mode binary's Zilsd alignment assumptions. This is a latent risk — if someone builds OpenSBI for an RV32+Zilsd platform and the S-mode kernel is compiled with -mzilsd-word-align on strict-align hardware, nothing in the build catches the inconsistency.

